.TH PWD 1 "March, 2026"

.SH NAME
\fBpwd\fP \- print the name of the current working directory

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpwd\fP [\fB\-L\fP|\fB\-P\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Uses
.IR getcwd (3)
to obtain the current working directory and prints it, followed by a line feed,
to the standard output.

.SS Options

.TP
.B \-L
Print the value of the \fB$PWD\fP environment variable, if it is sensible and
represents the current directory.

.TP
.B \-P
Print the result of
.IR getcwd (3).
This is the default.

.SH NOTES
.IR esh (1)
does not have a built\-in \fBpwd\fP, so you're always going to get this one
unless you use a different shell.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getcwd (3),
.BR sys_getcwd (9)
